---
import { withBase } from '../../data/url';
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CaseHeader from '../../components/CaseHeader.astro';
import CaseSections from '../../components/CaseSections.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import MarginNote from '../../components/MarginNote.astro';
import EditView from '../../components/EditView.astro';
import EnFace from '../../components/EnFace.astro';
import Persona from '../../components/Persona.astro';
import Pager from '../../components/Pager.astro';

const project = (await getEntry('projects', 'alfredo-voice-services'))!;

const styleGuide = [
  { rule: 'Say the time of day in long form.', en: 'It’s ten a.m.', es: 'Son las diez de la mañana.', no: 'Son las diez a.m.' },
  { rule: 'Use non-colloquial language.', en: 'Go ahead and enter that number.', es: 'Continúa e ingresa ese número.', no: 'Dale ahora y pon ese número.' },
  { rule: 'Capture emotion the way the original does.', en: 'That worked!', es: '¡Funcionó!', no: 'Eso fue bueno.' },
  { rule: 'The subject must be clear from the phrase alone.', en: 'Savvy choice.', es: 'Buena elección.', no: 'Muy sensato.' },
];
---
<Base title={project.data.title} description={project.data.summary} current="work">
  <article class="wrap alfredo">
    <CaseHeader project={project} />
    <CaseSections>

    <Section id="context" title="Context">
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <p>
          Interactive voice response (IVR) systems are standard for customer service in the U.S. Most are in English, with a growing
          number in Spanish.
        </p>
        <p>
          Twenty percent of this service’s users had already chosen Spanish, through their online account or the app. The job was to
          give them, in Spanish, the same experience the IVR gave English speakers.
        </p>
      </div>
      <MarginNote label="About the 20%">That figure is the share of users who already preferred Spanish. It describes the audience, not a result of this project.</MarginNote>
    </Section>

    <Section id="personas" title="Personas">
      <div class="col-wide two-up">
        <Persona
          name="José"
          age={38}
          status="Married"
          occupation="Architect"
          location="Houston, Texas"
          quote="When I hear there is a Spanish option, it’s a sigh of relief! I can be myself for the whole conversation."
          bio="Costa Rican; moved to Texas for work. Has a wife and three kids in Texas; the rest of his family is in San José. Travels around the city for work and keeps in touch with coworkers by calls and texts."
          needs="Reliable contact with family in both countries, and with colleagues."
          pains="Waiting forever in customer service lines, and miscommunication, since English is his second language."
        />
        <Persona
          kind="Product persona: the voice"
          name="Alfredo"
          age={35}
          status="Single"
          occupation="Customer service representative"
          location="Miami, Florida"
          quote="How may we help you today?"
          bio="A Latin American customer service agent, ready to help. He understands how much communication matters to Spanish speakers, especially when family is far away."
        />
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section id="brainstorming" title="Brainstorming">
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <p>
          As a team we went to the whiteboard to pin down who the English voice was. Through a lot of mapping, and by going back to the
          original English documentation from the early 2000s, we created Alfredo.
        </p>
        <p>
          The call flows also had to change for what Spanish speakers asked for. They requested long-distance international service
          significantly more often than English speakers did, for example.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section id="voice" title="Voice and tone" kicker="Voice design">
      <dl class="col-wide voice-attrs">
        <div>
          <dt>Latin American accent</dt>
          <dd>Specifically a Miami accent. Miami is a major U.S. city with a large Spanish-speaking population, and the accent is neutral and intelligible to Spanish speakers everywhere.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>North American vocabulary</dt>
          <dd>Alfredo tells time on the 12-hour clock, knows all 50 states and their best-known cities, and uses U.S. dollars.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Minimal slang</dt>
          <dd>Neutral vocabulary, as you’d expect from someone living in North America. Any slang must be understood across the wider Spanish-speaking world, never local.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Informal</dt>
          <dd>Alfredo uses <span lang="es"><em>tú</em></span> (with <span lang="es"><em>tu, tus, tuyo</em></span>…), which makes him friendly and approachable.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </Section>

    <Section id="style-guide" title="Style guide" kicker="Writing">
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <p>
          The voice and tone went into the style guide, along with cases that needed more guidance. Time of day, for example, has
          specific words for noon and midnight.
        </p>
      </div>
      <EnFace
        caption="Style guide examples"
        columns={[
          { key: 'rule', label: 'Standard' },
          { key: 'en', label: 'Original English', lang: 'en', muted: true },
          { key: 'es', label: 'Correct Spanish', lang: 'es' },
          { key: 'no', label: 'Incorrect', lang: 'es', strike: true },
        ]}
        rows={styleGuide}
      />
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <p>
          The guide also lists English words accepted in Spanish interactions: technology terms like <em>hotspot</em>, <em>wireless</em> and <em>Wi-Fi</em>, and proper names of places (Houston) and brands (Apple). It follows the company’s
          branding too, including the names of services, such as <span lang="es"><em>hotspot móvil</em></span>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section id="sms" title="SMS" kicker="Writing">
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <p>
          I edited the text messages so they follow the same style as the audio, fixing misspellings and awkward phrasing.
          Here are the individual edits.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ol class="col-wide sms-edits">
        <li>
          <p class="meta sms-edits__where">Cancel notifications</p>
          <EditView size="small" parts={[{ del: 'STOP' }, { ins: 'NO' }]} lang="es" />
          <p class="sms-edits__why">The client wanted a Spanish version of “STOP” in fewer characters. “NO” is the shortest word. To subscribe again, people can text “SI” or “SÍ”.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="meta sms-edits__where">Cancel notifications</p>
          <EditView size="small" parts={[{ del: 'Mande texto' }, { ins: 'enviar' }]} lang="es" />
          <p class="sms-edits__why">“Mande texto” sounds odd and formal. I made the line informal and used a form of <span lang="es"><em>enviar</em></span>, which fits texting better.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="meta sms-edits__where">Registration</p>
          <EditView size="small" parts={[{ t: 'JUAN …' }]} lang="es" caption="Moved to the start" />
          <p class="sms-edits__why">The customer’s name moved to the start, to address the person first and then let them focus on registering.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="meta sms-edits__where">Registration</p>
          <EditView size="small" parts={[{ del: 'haga clic' }]} lang="es" />
          <p class="sms-edits__why">Removed: it’s formal, and people don’t click on a phone.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="meta sms-edits__where">Registration</p>
          <EditView size="small" parts={[{ del: 'vinculo' }, { ins: 'enlace' }]} lang="es" />
          <p class="sms-edits__why"><span lang="es"><em>Enlace</em></span> is the more widely accepted word for “link”, and with the link right there the word was redundant. The original was also missing its accent (<span lang="es"><em>vínculo</em></span>).</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="meta sms-edits__where">Registration</p>
          <EditView size="small" parts={[{ ins: 'aquí' }]} lang="es" />
          <p class="sms-edits__why">Added to point to the link below.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </Section>

    <Section id="retrospective" title="Retrospective">
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <p>
          People have chosen the Spanish IVR, and it gives them an experience as close as possible to what English speakers get.
          The project was still in progress at the time, but it was already a reminder that localization belongs at the start
          of a product: it changes flows, content length and any structure that works differently in another language.
        </p>
        <p>
          Personally, I enjoyed coaching the voice actors, seeing the SMS and the IVR work hand in hand, and using SSML to change the
          prosody of the recordings.
        </p>
        <p>
          Related: my 2021 talk <a href={withBase("/archive/#talk-chatbot-summit")}>“Localization and Latin America”</a> at the Ibero-American
          Chatbot Summit.
        </p>
      </div>
      <MarginNote label="No screens">This is an audio and text product, so the examples above stand in for screenshots.</MarginNote>
    </Section>
    </CaseSections>
  </article>
  <Pager id={project.id} />
</Base>

<style>
  .voice-attrs {
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 0 var(--gutter);
  }
  @media (min-width: 760px) { .voice-attrs { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); } }
  .voice-attrs div { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding: var(--space-3) 0 var(--space-4); }
  .voice-attrs dt { font-size: var(--step-2); line-height: 1.15; margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }
  .voice-attrs dd { margin: 0; font-size: var(--step--1); max-width: 44ch; }

  .sms-edits {
    list-style: none;
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 0 var(--gutter);
  }
  @media (min-width: 760px) { .sms-edits { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); } }
  .sms-edits li { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding: var(--space-3) 0 var(--space-4); }
  .sms-edits :global(.edit-view) { margin: var(--space-2) 0; }
  .sms-edits__where { margin: 0; }
  .sms-edits__why { font-size: var(--step--1); margin: 0; }
</style>
